INT. PASCAL HOUSE - ATTIC - AFTERNOON

Slanted ceiling. Cardboard boxes marked in a dead man's handwriting. A mannequin with a hat on it. A small round window, dusty light.

LEONA, 40s, in jeans, kneels over a trunk. Her brother PASCAL, 40s, in a work jacket, stands at the top of the stairs with his arms folded.

LEONA
You took Dad's watch.

PASCAL
He gave it to me.

LEONA
In the hospital. When he couldn't talk.

PASCAL
He pointed.

LEONA
At you. Or at the window.

PASCAL
At me.

Leona lifts a sweater from the trunk. A box of old photographs falls out. She catches it. Pascal takes a step forward, both hands out, and they hold the box between them for a moment, each with a grip on a corner.

PASCAL (CONT'D)
Give me that.

LEONA
You give it.

They tug. The lid pops. Photographs scatter across the floor, and both stop, crouch, and begin gathering them without a word.

LEONA (CONT'D)
This one. Do you remember?

PASCAL
Don't.

LEONA
You were nine. You had that haircut.

PASCAL
He cut it himself, with kitchen scissors.

LEONA
You cried for a week.

PASCAL
I did not.

LEONA
You hid in the car.

PASCAL
Okay. I hid in the car.

She hands him the photograph. He studies it for a long time. His thumb moves over the face.

PASCAL (CONT'D)
Take the watch.

LEONA
I don't want the watch.

PASCAL
Then why are we here?

LEONA
Because I don't want to be here alone.

He sits on the trunk. She sits beside him, and they look at the photographs spread across the boards like leaves.
